// @test: typecheck-fail
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: types, records, literals, self
// @expected-error: "Missing required field"

// `Self { … }` is the dominant shape of a constructor, and it was the
// one shape no check reached.
//
// Five forms that differ ONLY in the spelling of the literal's head, all
// measured on one binary:
//
//     fn f() -> Self { Self { a: 1 } }   accepted, t.b read 0.0
//     fn f() -> Two  { Self { a: 1 } }   accepted
//     fn f() -> Self { Two  { a: 1 } }   REFUSED
//     let x: Self = Self { a: 1 };       accepted
//     let x: Two  = Self { a: 1 };       accepted
//
// So the miss was the SPELLING, in every position — not the position, as
// the defect's own filing predicted.
//
// The parser emits `Self` as an ordinary `Name("Self")`, reserving the
// `SelfValue` segment for the lowercase `self`.  `Name("Self")` fell
// through to the ordinary type-name lookup and found the placeholder a
// `define_type("Self", …)` installs — a name pointing at itself — so the
// field-map probe missed and the literal took a structural fallback that
// accepts anything.
//
// A first fix matched the `SelfValue` segment and was measurably inert
// for exactly that reason.

type Two is { a: Int, b: Int };

implement Two {
    public fn new() -> Self {
        Self { a: 1 }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let t = Two.new();
    print(f"a={t.a} b={t.b}");
}
